WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.640
What if just one
lifestyle change

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.440
could help you
avoid getting cancer,

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.280
or diabetes,
or heart disease,

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:10.640
or high blood pressure?

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.000
This one change could cut
your risk of chronic disease

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.400
and add years to your life.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.520
Well, the simple solution to
so many of these problems

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.840
is to eat a healthy diet.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.280
In other words, one centered
around whole plant foods.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.800
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.200
I'm your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.400
As you know, I like to
put things to the test.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.160
And today we’re going to ask
YOU to do that with your diet,

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.080
using the phytochemical index.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.080
Anything with the word "chemical"
in it may sound a little fishy,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.720
but phytochemicals can
be good for you.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.880
Here's the first story.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.560
How do you rate the
quality of people's diets?

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.960
Well, what could be more nutrient
dense than a vegetarian diet?

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.363
Indeed, if you compare the quality
of vegetarian diets compared to

00:01:02.363 --> 00:01:06.240
nonvegetarian diets, the more
plant-based diets do tend to win out,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.120
and the higher diet quality
in vegetarian diets may in fact

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.320
help explain better improvements
in health outcomes.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.160
However, vegetarians appeared
to have higher refined-grain intake,

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.560
eating more foods like
white rice and white bread

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.120
that have been stripped
of much of their nutrition.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.920
So just because you're
eating a vegetarian diet

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.800
doesn't mean you're necessarily
eating optimally healthy.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.520
Those familiar with the science

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.800
know the primary health importance
of eating whole plant foods.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.040
So how about a scoring system that
just adds up how many cups of fruit,

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:40.080
vegetables, whole grains, and beans,
split peas, chickpeas and lentils,

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.600
and how many ounces of nuts
and seeds per thousand calories,

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.840
with or without
counting white potatoes.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.600
Well, just looking at the total intake
of whole plant foods doesn't mean

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.320
you're not also stuffing
doughnuts into your face.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.600
So, you could imagine
proportional intake measures,

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.720
based on calories or weight

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.000
to determine the proportion of
your diet that's whole plant foods.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.120
In that case, you'd get docked
points if you eat things like

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.960
animal-derived foods —
meat, dairy, or eggs —

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:06.560
or added sugars and fats.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.160
My favorite proportional intake measure
is McCarty's Phytochemical Index.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.200
I love it because its sheer simplicity,

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.200
defined as the percent of dietary
calories derived from foods

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.800
rich in phytochemicals.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.880
So, it's a score from 0 to 100:

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:25.760
the percentage of your calories
that are derived from foods

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.680
rich in phytochemicals, which
are biologically active substances

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.480
naturally found in plants
that may be contributing

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.840
to many of the health benefits obtained
from eating whole plant foods.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.240
I mean, monitoring phytochemical
intake in the clinical setting

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.360
could have great utility in
assisting people with optimizing

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.240
their dietary intake for optimal
health and disease prevention.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.440
However, the quantification
of phytochemicals in foods

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.360
or tissue samples is impractical,
expensive, laborious; but hey,

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.920
this concept of a phytochemical index
score could be an alternative,

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.120
simple method of monitoring
phytochemical intake.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.560
Theoretically, a whole food,
plant-based or vegan diet

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.440
that excluded refined grains,
white potatoes, hard liquors,

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.600
and added sugars and oils could
achieve a perfect score of 100,

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.920
whereas, sadly, the most current
American diets might be lucky to hit 20.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.880
Let's see what's going on. In 1998,
our shopping baskets were filled

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.560
with about 20% whole plant foods, and
more recently that has actually shrunk.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.080
Wouldn't it be interesting if researchers
used this index to try to correlate it

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.600
with health outcomes?
That's exactly what they did.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.600
Look, we know that studies have
demonstrated vegetarian diets

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.560
have a protective association
with weight and body mass index;

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.000
five dozen studies have shown
that vegetarians had significantly

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.720
lower weight and BMI
compared with non-vegetarians.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.840
And even more studies show that
high intakes of fruits, vegetables,

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.080
whole grains and beans
may be protective,

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.000
regardless of meat consumption.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.080
So researchers wanted to use an index
that gave points for whole plant foods.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.200
They used a phytochemical index
and tracked people's weight

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.080
over the next few years, which
again, I profiled in my video,

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:09.760
"Calculate Your Healthy Eating Score"

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.440
on a scale of 0 to 100, simply
reflecting what percentage

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.080
of your diet is
whole plant foods.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.000
And even though the healthiest eating
tier only averaged a score of about 40,

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.360
which meant the bulk of their diet
was still made up of processed foods

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.880
and animal products, just making
whole plant foods a substantial

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.480
portion of the diet may prevent
weight gain and decrease body fat.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:31.840
So it's not all or nothing.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.520
Any steps we can make to
increase our whole plant food intake

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:36.800
may be beneficial.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.600
A couple more studies
have been performed since,

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.520
and they all point in the same
direction: about a third of the odds

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.360
of abdominal obesity at
higher healthy plant intake

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.400
and significantly lower
odds of high triglycerides.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.840
So the index may be a useful
dietary target for weight loss,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.320
where there's less focus on calorie
intake and more on increasing intake

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.600
of these high-nutrient,
lower-calorie foods over time.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:04.160
The latest such study,
published 2020,

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.640
suggests the same is
true for childhood obesity.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.200
Even at the same weight,
the same amount of belly fat,

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.680
those eating plant-based have
higher insulin sensitivity,

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.880
meaning the insulin they make
works better in their body,

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.480
perhaps thanks to the compounds
in plants that alleviate inflammation

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:23.840
and quench free radicals.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.440
And indeed, things seemed to improve
as people ate more and more plants.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.440
No wonder researchers found
91% lower odds of prediabetes

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.120
for people getting more than half their
calories from healthy plant foods,

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:39.600
significantly lower odds of metabolic
syndrome and high blood pressure,

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.480
only about half the odds of being
diagnosed with hypertension

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.840
over a 3-year period among
those eating more plants.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.920
Even mental health was impacted.
About 80% less depression,

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.080
2/3 less anxiety, 70% less
psychological distress.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.280
What about the Dietary Phytochemical
Index and benign breast diseases,

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:03.280
such as fibrocystic diseases,
ductal ectasia, fatty necrosis,

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.360
and all sorts of benign tumors?

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:09.920
The same thing was found: 70%
lower odds of those diseases.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.640
OK, but what about breast cancer?

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.120
A higher intake of healthy plant
foods was indeed associated

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.920
with a lower risk of breast cancer,
even after controlling for

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.200
a long list of other factors,
and not just by a little bit.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:26.960
Eating twice the proportion of plants
compared to the standard American diet

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.600
was linked to more than 90%
lower odds of breast cancer.

00:06:33.045 --> 00:06:36.068
What evidence do we
have that scoring higher

00:06:36.068 --> 00:06:39.680
is actually better?
Let's find out.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.640
A few years ago, a proposal was
published for a healthy eating index,

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.360
and I thought it might be interesting to
look at the latest USDA dietary survey

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:52.560
to see how the standard
American diet is doing.

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:57.680
The index is simple;
it’s a score of 0 to 100,

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.720
which simply represents the percent
of dietary calories derived from foods

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:04.400
rich in phytochemicals.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.800
Phytochemical is just a more
technical term for phytonutrient,

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.760
since nutrient implies essential for life,
whereas phytochemicals are merely

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.840
essential for a long, healthy life.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.280
So if 1% of our diet is composed
of phytonutrient-rich food,

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:23.280
our diet gets a score of 1.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.880
If that’s where half of our calories
come from, then we score 50.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:30.960
And if that’s all we eat,
we can max out at 100; 100%.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:36.720
How are Jane and Joe Sixpack doing?

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.560
Here is the latest data on
the standard American diet:

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.520
3% of calories come
from beans and nuts;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:48.080
3% from fruit;
5% is vegetables;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:52.560
23% from grain;
17% is added sugars,

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:54.400
like candy and soda
and other junk;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.520
23% comes from added fats—
butter, margarine, oil, shortening;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:03.040
and 26% of the American diet
is meat, dairy, and eggs.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:09.600
For the healthy eating index, we only
get to count phytonutrient-rich foods,

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.840
since they’re the ones
most associated with

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:14.880
chronic disease prevention,
treatment, and cure.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:20.400
So, first off, the reason they’re called
phytonutrients is that, by definition,

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:24.640
they are found in plants—derived from
the Greek word “phyton,” for plant.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:30.240
So, automatically we
start with a score of 74.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.120
Neither lard nor candy are
phytonutrient-rich either,

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.840
so taking away the added fats and oils,
we’re down to 34.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:44.240
But the grain category
is a combination

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:46.960
of both whole grains—
rich in phytonutrients—

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:50.880
and refined grains, which had the
phytonutrients largely removed.

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:56.480
Only 4% of the American diet
is composed of whole grains—

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:59.440
like oats, barley,
whole wheat, brown rice—

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.680
and the rest is highly
processed garbage,

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:03.600
like white flour and cornstarch.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:09.200
Yikes, down to 15!
But it gets worse.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:14.480
2/3 of our vegetables are white
potatoes, half of which are potato chips.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:18.880
The average American eats
23 calories of potato chips every day.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:22.560
But none of the
white potato products count,

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.760
since they contain relatively
few phytonutrients.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:32.000
Similarly, a third of our fruit calories
are low-phytonutrient juices,

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:37.440
and so the typical American diet
rates 12, out of 100.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:42.480
So on a scale of like 1 to 10,
we get about a 1.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:45.920
How do you score a perfect 10?

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:52.560
“Theoretically, a vegan diet
that excluded refined grains,

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:57.440
potato products, hard liquors,
and added sugars and oils could have a

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:00.320
[perfect dietary index rating] of 100.

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:05.600
Sadly, the [score] of most current
American diets would be unlikely to be

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:09.360
as high as 20
[yeah we wish it were 20!]

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:14.160
—which means that there [is]
quite ample room for improvement.”

